A||||6||mshill||Too many coolant temperature sensors...||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||02-18-2000||11:59 PM||||Ok, I have an 86 V6 and am in the process of the engine rebuild and starting to put things back together.  I have four (yes that's 4) temperature sending units:<P>1) Temp guage/idiot light Sensor on corner of  head. (3 contacts).<P>2) Radiator fan temperature switch on the intake manifold just to the right of the thermostat housing. (1 contact)<P>3) ECM Coolant Temperature Sensor directly under the thermostat housing. (2 contacts, yellow and black wires).<P><BR>Now there is yet another one, THIS IS THE ONE THAT I DON'T KNOW WHERE IT GOES:<P>4) On top of the intake manifold right next to the radiator fan switch (#2 above).  It has a very long sensor shaft.  Two connectors connect to the harness with red/white and orange wires.<P>I cannot find any mention of this sensor or any red/white and orange wire connections in my Haynes manual.  If I want to replace this what do I order?<P> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||02-19-2000||12:26 AM||||Look on page 279 of your Haynes manual. Top right area The item is (I think) the cold start injector switch. The haynes manual shows a purple/white and tan the 1986 Factory service manual shows red/white and tan.  I am not sure if this is the sensor your looking for. I also don't know which book has the right wire color. Neither book shows a pic of the sensor. I cannot look at mine due to EXCESSIVE SNOW. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Luperman||02-19-2000||12:56 AM||||hey I just had that one out ,actually I just had all 4 of them out in my car 86 v-6 and oreif is right it is the cold start injector.||||reg||10
Z||000003||mshill||02-19-2000||01:39 AM||||Thanks very much!!!!!<P>Makes you wonder, how come, if these things are computer controlled, they couldn't run everything of off one sensor going to the ECM.  Since the ECM knows what the temperature is it should be able to 1) drive the idiot light, 2) turn on the radiator fan, 3) turn on the cold start injector.  It could probably even drive the gauge.  Why not, it is just another electrical signal.  It is just a couple more bits off the microcontroller and a little programming.  At $15 a sensor, they could have saved $30-45 in parts easily.||||reg||4
Z||000004||theogre||02-19-2000||11:27 AM||||GM deleted the whole cold start rig and reprogrammed the ECM. I don't know in what year.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tiacPaul||02-19-2000||11:45 PM||||I agree completely with the one sensor idea.  I went through all kinds of trouble replaceing my thee.  Gague, fan, and com.  Who's dumb idea was that!<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000006||mwbackus||02-20-2000||01:20 AM||||My 88 V6 has a cold start valve/injector. <BR>I recently removed it for testing and cleaning. It hides behind the distributor and is simply a solenoid valve with a jet.  I also suspect that the o-ring used to seat it in the manifold is quite prone to leakage.<P>Mike||||reg||1
